  "_notes": [
    "\uD83D\uDEA9 v1.10 (879 §1 / §2.7) — header.coupons[] is an ARRAY, because the engine holds a LIST and applyCoupon's duplicate check is per-code, so a second, DIFFERENT coupon is accepted. `[]` on an order with none, never null.",
    "`code` is what the AGENT typed. The engine attributes a coupon's money by the campaign MATERIAL (PromotionCouponCode = COUPT173 for a coupon keyed as SAVE20 — SIS.Pos's own CouponAttributionEndToEndTests pins that in those words) and keeps the typed code in a private list with no accessor, so the sidecar carries the label. `amount` is PromotionCouponDiscount summed over the lines carrying the material, negative.",
    "\uD83D\uDEA9 removeCoupon REVERSES FIRST and voids only if the reverse landed (issue 211's till invariant). VoidLineAsync on a COUP line drops the bonus buy correctly since 581, but CollectReversalContexts() is reached from VoidTransactionAsync ONLY — so a bare line-void would drop the discount and leave the customer's coupon SPENT. A refused reverse answers COUPON_REVERSAL_REFUSED and leaves the order byte-identical, which is the refusal's whole meaning.",
    "capabilities.canApplyCoupon is canAddItem's OWN predicate (§3), not a similar one: the redeem stamps storeCode: plant into the coupon ledger permanently, and 129's documented-non-event ruling is true of the ORDER and false of that row. canRemoveCoupon additionally needs the order to hold one AND the reversal leg to be reachable — the client deliberately does not derive it from coupons.length.",
    "⚠ A SHUT GATE IS NOT A SHUT CHIP: header.coupons[] projects even when canApplyCoupon is false, because the order may hold a coupon the agent has to read out on a call where a new one may not be applied.",
    "\uD83D\uDEA9 addItem of a coupon SKU is ITEM_NOT_SELLABLE, and the assertion is written so nobody later \"fixes\" it by allowing it (871's negative pattern). ⚖ Recorded fairly: the alternative outcome was a refusal by the no-price scan back-out AddCouponAsync sidesteps, and which of the two happened depended on whether the campaign SKU carried a price. Both are wrong; refusing by NAME means the outcome no longer varies with master data."
  ]
}
